---
import Base from "@/layouts/Base.astro";

/**
 * `/assistant/` — the on-ramp from "circal keeps a live copy of my calendar"
 * to "an assistant can read it". Static markup, no island, same reasoning as
 * `manifesto.astro`: this is a page you read once and leave, and shipping
 * React to print a JSON snippet would cost more than the snippet.
 *
 * Two doors, because the Settings panel keeps at most one live copy at a
 * time: the mirror file on this computer, or the encrypted cloud copy. The
 * server (`circal-mcp`) serves either; the page's job is to route the reader
 * to the right one and be honest about what each costs.
 *
 * The file half cannot be smarter than the platform lets it be. A
 * `FileSystemFileHandle` exposes `.name` and nothing else — no path, by
 * design, because a page knowing where on your disk a file sits is the exact
 * privacy leak the File System Access API exists to prevent. So this page
 * cannot look up the path and cannot pre-fill it anywhere; it can only point
 * at `circal-mcp --find`, the one thing standing outside the browser that is
 * allowed to look. The cloud half needs no path at all, and its sign-in has
 * to be a terminal's for a harder reason: the passphrase derives the key on
 * the machine that will hold it, and no web flow may ever see it.
 *
 * Linked from Settings → Mirror and Settings → Cloud ("Set up an assistant")
 * once either copy is live, and from the README. Not linked from the app
 * shell itself: it is setup, not a feature, and the instrument has nowhere
 * to put a link to it.
 */
---

<Base
  title="Set up an assistant · circal"
  description="Three steps to let an MCP-speaking assistant read and write your calendar, through its mirror file or its encrypted cloud copy."
  scroll
>
  <main class="mx-auto w-full max-w-[36rem] px-6 pt-14 pb-24 sm:pt-20">
    <a
      href="/"
      class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-ink-2 transition-colors hover:text-ink"
      aria-label="Open circal"
    >
      <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" class="size-[15px]" aria-hidden="true">
        <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10.5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"
        ></circle>
      </svg>
      <span class="text-[15px] font-medium tracking-[-0.02em]">circal</span>
    </a>

    <h1
      class="mt-14 text-[34px] leading-[1.08] font-medium tracking-[-0.035em] text-balance text-ink sm:text-[44px]"
    >
      Let an assistant read your calendar.
    </h1>

    <p class="mt-5 text-[15px] leading-[1.6] text-ink-3">Three steps, one of them in a terminal.</p>

    <p class="mt-10 text-[17px] leading-[1.75] text-ink-2">
      Your calendar lives in this browser, and circal keeps at most one live copy of it: a mirror
      file on your computer, or the encrypted cloud copy, whichever you switched on in Settings. An
      assistant reads and writes whichever copy you have, through a small server that runs on your
      own machine. With the file, nothing touches the internet at all. With the cloud copy, the
      assistant works on the same sealed record the app syncs with, and the seal holds both ways: it
      is opened and closed on your machine, with a key made from your passphrase, and the server
      behind it never holds anything it can read.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-12">
      <h2 class="label text-ink-2">What you need</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-[1.7] text-ink-2">
        Node.js 22 or later, from <a
          href="https://nodejs.org"
          class="text-ink underline decoration-hairline underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-ink-2"
          >nodejs.org</a
        >, and an assistant that speaks MCP: Claude Desktop, Claude Code, Cursor, VS Code and Zed
        all do. One step below needs a terminal, and there is no way around it: outside the browser
        is the only place allowed to find your file, and the only place your passphrase may go.
      </p>
    </div>

    {
      /* `flex flex-col gap-*` rather than `space-y-*`: this page was the only
        place in the app reaching for `space-y`, and the utility is not in the
        generated stylesheet at all, so every gap it claimed to set measured
        zero. `gap` cannot fail quietly the same way. */
    }
    <ol class="mt-12 flex flex-col gap-10">
      <li>
        <h2 class="label text-ink-2">Step 1 · Connect the server</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-[1.7] text-ink-2">
          <span class="text-ink">Using the cloud copy?</span> Sign the server in, once per machine:
        </p>
        <pre
          class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-[10px] border border-hairline bg-surface-2 p-3.5"><code class="text-[13px] leading-[1.6] text-ink font-mono">npx -y circal-mcp login</code></pre>
        <p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-[1.7] text-ink-2">
          Three prompts: your email, the code it receives, your passphrase. The passphrase is never
          stored and never sent; the key it derives stays on this machine, in a file only your user
          account can read, and the cloud copy stays sealed to its server exactly as before. The
          session renews itself, so this is a one-time step. <code
            class="font-mono text-[13px] text-ink-2">npx -y circal-mcp logout</code
          > withdraws the key immediately.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[15px] leading-[1.7] text-ink-2">
          <span class="text-ink">Mirroring to a file instead?</span> circal can't tell you where the file
          is. The browser only ever hands the page a handle to it, never a path, on purpose: a web page
          is not supposed to know your filesystem layout. Run this instead:
        </p>
        <pre
          class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-[10px] border border-hairline bg-surface-2 p-3.5"><code class="text-[13px] leading-[1.6] text-ink font-mono">npx -y circal-mcp --find</code></pre>
        <p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-[1.7] text-ink-2">
          It searches the usual places, prints the path to every valid mirror file it finds, and a
          config block underneath ready to paste into the next step.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <h2 class="label text-ink-2">Step 2 · Tell your assistant</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-[1.7] text-ink-2">
          Most MCP clients read a JSON file. For the cloud copy, put this in it, exactly as printed,
          no path needed:
        </p>
        <pre
          class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-[10px] border border-hairline bg-surface-2 p-3.5"><code class="text-[13px] leading-[1.6] text-ink font-mono">{`{
  "mcpServers": {
    "circal": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "circal-mcp", "--cloud"]
    }
  }
}`}</code></pre>
        <p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-[1.7] text-ink-2">
          For a mirror file, the last two args are <code class="font-mono text-[13px] text-ink-2"
            >"--file", "/absolute/path/to/circal.json"</code
          > instead, with the path from Step 1 in place of the placeholder.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[15px] leading-[1.7] text-ink-2">
          <span class="text-ink">Claude Desktop</span> reads it from Settings → Developer → Edit Config,
          which opens <code class="font-mono text-[13px] text-ink-2"
            >claude_desktop_config.json</code
          >.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-[1.7] text-ink-2">
          <span class="text-ink">Cursor</span> reads it from <code
            class="font-mono text-[13px] text-ink-2">~/.cursor/mcp.json</code
          >, or <code class="font-mono text-[13px] text-ink-2">.cursor/mcp.json</code> in one project
          only.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-[1.7] text-ink-2">
          <span class="text-ink">Claude Code</span> is easier from the command line, no file to find or
          edit:
        </p>
        <pre
          class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-[10px] border border-hairline bg-surface-2 p-3.5"><code class="text-[13px] leading-[1.6] text-ink font-mono">claude mcp add circal -- npx -y circal-mcp --cloud</code></pre>
      </li>

      <li>
        <h2 class="label text-ink-2">Step 3 · Restart it and ask</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-[1.7] text-ink-2">
          Restart the assistant so it picks up the new server, then talk to it the way you would
          talk to a person:
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-4 flex flex-col gap-2">
          <li
            class="rounded-[10px] border border-hairline bg-surface p-3 text-[14px] text-ink-2 italic"
          >
            "What's on Thursday?"
          </li>
          <li
            class="rounded-[10px] border border-hairline bg-surface p-3 text-[14px] text-ink-2 italic"
          >
            "Find me an hour free tomorrow afternoon."
          </li>
          <li
            class="rounded-[10px] border border-hairline bg-surface p-3 text-[14px] text-ink-2 italic"
          >
            "Book dentist next Tuesday at 2."
          </li>
          <li
            class="rounded-[10px] border border-hairline bg-surface p-3 text-[14px] text-ink-2 italic"
          >
            "Move my standup to 10."
          </li>
        </ul>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <div class="mt-14">
      <h2 class="label text-ink-2">What it will not do</h2>
      <ul class="mt-3 flex flex-col gap-2.5 text-[15px] leading-[1.7] text-ink-2">
        <li class="flex gap-2.5">
          <span class="text-ink-4" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
          <span>It will not touch a subscribed calendar. Those stay read-only.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-2.5">
          <span class="text-ink-4" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
          <span>
            It will not guess what "delete my standup" means for a repeating event. It asks which
            occurrences you meant, every time.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-2.5">
          <span class="text-ink-4" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
          <span>
            It will not write at all if it is running in a different timezone from the one your
            calendar was written in.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-2.5">
          <span class="text-ink-4" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
          <span>
            It will not create the cloud copy, or an account. Signing up, choosing the passphrase
            and the first upload all belong to Settings → Cloud; the assistant only ever opens a box
            that already exists.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-14 border-t border-hairline pt-8">
      <h2 class="label text-ink-2">The trade</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-[1.7] text-ink-2">
        While a mirror file is connected, anything on your computer that can read files can read and
        change your calendar. Turning the mirror off in Settings stops it.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-[1.7] text-ink-2">
        While the server is signed in to the cloud copy, this machine holds a key that can read and
        change the calendar on every device on the account, so anyone who can read your files holds
        it too. <code class="font-mono text-[13px] text-ink-2">circal-mcp logout</code> withdraws it now,
        not at the next launch.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-16 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3 border-t border-hairline pt-8">
      <a
        href="/"
        class="inline-flex h-9 items-center rounded-[9px] bg-ink px-4 text-[13.5px] font-medium text-void transition-opacity hover:opacity-90"
      >
        Open circal
      </a>
      <a
        href="/manifesto/"
        class="inline-flex h-9 items-center rounded-[9px] border border-hairline px-4 text-[13.5px] font-medium text-ink-2 transition-colors hover:text-ink"
      >
        Read the manifesto
      </a>
    </div>
  </main>
</Base>
